<!-- Generated by `rotproof create`. Do not edit: `rotproof check` fails when this file differs from what `rotproof create` writes. -->

# Running Rotproof

The command to run next is the one `rotproof check` and `rotproof init` print, and `rotproof <command> --help` says what a
command reads and writes. This page only says how to judge what they print. The rules of the project's layers and
records are in `.rotproof/AGENTS.md`.

## When to run which command

- **`rotproof check`: before you hand over a change, and whenever `.config/rotproof.toml`, the code or `docs/` changed.**
  It is the project's CI. Read what it says it did not check: a part it skipped has not passed.
- **`rotproof create`: when a project starts, and after you changed `.config/rotproof.toml` on purpose.** Run it with no
  `--yes` first and read the layers it lists: it makes each layer that is neither present nor declared in `absent`. List
  a layer the project does not want in `absent` and run it again; pass `--yes` only when the list is what the project
  should have. Never run it to upgrade: that is `init`.
- **`rotproof baseline`: only when the user asked you to freeze the violations there are, and only once.** It writes
  `.config/rotproof-baseline.toml` from what `rotproof check` fails on now and refuses while `moving` in
  `.config/rotproof.toml` lists a path. After that, `rotproof baseline --prune` is the only run: it removes the entries
  that match nothing, after you fixed a violation. Never run it to make a new violation pass, never write the file
  again and never raise its `count`: the check fails on more entries than the count, and the diff shows a file written
  again.
- **`rotproof index`: after a knowledge document is added, changed or deprecated,** and, when a unit is shared, after
  the imports between units change (it rewrites `.rotproof/readers.md`). Never edit the files it writes by hand. A
  document it lists as left out is a document to fix, not an index to edit.
- **`rotproof follows knowledge/<slug>.md <key>...`: only after you have read the document against the code it follows.**
  The pin says the document was reviewed. When `rotproof check` says a followed key changed, read the document and the
  change, correct the document if it no longer holds (or deprecate it), and then pin. Never pin to make the failure go
  away, and never write a hash by hand. A key whose code is gone is edited out of the document by hand.
- **`rotproof new <slug>`: to start a knowledge document, in place of writing its frontmatter by hand.** It writes
  the frontmatter and nothing under it, with the `description` empty so that `rotproof check` fails until you write
  it, and prints the questions to ask. Ask them of the person, or take the answer from what the task decided, and write
  the body from the answers: never from what the code does, and never an empty heading. `--area` is needed when the
  project declares more than one area. `--alarm` starts an alarm, with `--match <string>` for each string of the
  output that stays when the wording changes; write `# When` and `# Do` as the output was seen. It pins nothing: once
  the body says what the code does, read the code against it and pin the keys with `rotproof follows`, as the pin says
  the document was reviewed. It never writes over a document, and does not write the index.
- **`rotproof log --title <task> --problem <text> --lesson <text>`: when a task is done, for its entry in
  `docs/log.md`.** It reads the day from the clock and puts the entry under that day's heading, so never write the
  day or the entry by hand, and the log need not be read first. `--problem` and `--lesson` are each needed: write
  `--problem None` when nothing went wrong, and give what the task left that is found nowhere else (what went wrong,
  the cause and the workaround; what to do differently, and where it became a rule). `--issue owner/repo#n` names the
  task's issue, when it has one. Repeat `--problem` or `--lesson` for a list; a line break in a value becomes a space.
- **`rotproof sizes`: before you refactor a part of the code, when you move code into the layers, and when the `sizes:`
  line of `rotproof check` changed.** `rotproof check` prints that line on every run and fails nothing for it; the
  command gives the numbers behind it (see "Reading the sizes").
- **`rotproof guide --stack <stack>`: to read the rules for a stack before a project exists.** In a project, the same
  text is `.rotproof/AGENTS.md`.

## Upgrading Rotproof

Upgrade in a commit of its own, so that what the new rules bring is read apart from the rest.

1. Change the version where the project pins it (its `README.md` says where), and install it. A Python project pins it in
   `dev` of `[dependency-groups]` in `pyproject.toml`, and locks again with its tool (`uv lock`, `poetry lock`,
   `pdm lock`) before it installs: a lock file made before still names the old version. Run `rotproof --version`: a
   binary left on the `PATH` from before would check the project by the old rules.
2. Run `rotproof init`. Until it has run, `rotproof check` fails. It applies what the new version brings to the files
   `rotproof create` wrote once, rewrites the files Rotproof generates, sets `files` in `.config/rotproof.toml` to the new
   version and never moves or rewrites a record. Read its output line by line:
   - `updated <file>: <name>`: an update was applied to a file. Read the diff of that file.
   - `added to .config/rotproof.toml: <field>`: a field the new version requires was added with a first value taken from
     the project. Read the comment above it, and check the value.
   - `move by hand: ...`: the new version changed what the layers are. `rotproof init` moves no code. Move it as the line
     says; `rotproof check` names what is not moved yet.
   - `needs a person: ...`: an update could not be applied by itself, `files` stays where it was and the command exits 2.
     Do what the line says by hand and run `rotproof init` again. Or decline the update by its name in `declined` in
     `.config/rotproof.toml`, which is a decision to keep that file as it is: decline only an update the project means
     not to take, and say so in your report. A name in `declined` that names nothing Rotproof offers fails
     `rotproof check`: take it out.
   - `ask the person: ...`: no line in the project's `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md` names `.rotproof/AGENTS.md` any more,
     and the project has not declined it. The command exits 2. Never answer it yourself: ask the person, then add the
     line it gives where they say, or add `guide-pointer` to `declined` if they say no.
   - `note: ...`: the map in the project's own `AGENTS.md` has gone behind. `rotproof init` never writes that file; edit
     the map by hand.
   - `drafted docs/knowledge/units.md`: a first picture of the units, made once from the code. Write over it with the
     picture that is intended: it follows the units, so `rotproof check` fails when one changes until the document is
     read against them and pinned again with `rotproof follows`.
3. Run `rotproof check`. A finding that a new rule brings is fixed like any other: it is part of the upgrade, and the
   reason to keep the upgrade apart from other work.

## Reading the sizes

`rotproof sizes` counts lines of code: a line with something on it that is not blank, not a comment (a Python docstring
is one) and not a test. Each part is a row with its files, its lines and its share of the total row; a level sits
under its layer and is counted in it too; `moving` and `unchecked` are rows of their own, counted in the total, so the
share of `moving` falls as code goes into the layers. With units, each unit has its own table. Under it, the longest
files of each layer. Rotproof states these numbers and judges none of them, and sets no line that is too little or
too much: that is for you to read, from what the project is for.

- **Compare a number with what its layer is for, not with another project.** What is normal depends on the project: a
  tool that reads files has more in `infrastructure.outbound` than in `infrastructure.inbound`, a server with no
  database the other way round. Ask the person before you restructure because of one share.
- **A layer with no code** (`sizes: no code in ...`): is the layer meant to be there? If not, declare it `absent`. If
  it is, look for its code elsewhere: in another layer, in `moving` or in `unchecked`.
- **`domain defines N types and no function`:** the types are there and nothing in `domain` runs. The rules of the
  program may sit outside `domain`, in `application` or in `infrastructure`: read where the decisions are made before
  you conclude anything. `domain holds ... lines of code and defines no function or type` is the same question for a
  `domain` of constants alone.
- **A layer far larger or smaller than its role suggests:** open its longest files and read what they are for. A file
  much longer than the others of its layer often holds more than one thing.
- **What changed since the last run says more than one value.** Compare the line with the one before it, across
  commits: a share that moves with a change is the change's effect, which is what to read.
